h condition characterized by high blood sugar levels, which significantly impacts how the body processes glucose. It often requires careful nutritional management and specific dietary protocols to control blood sugar levels and prevent complications. This condition is frequently associated with obesity, metabolic syndrome, and various health complications, including cardiovascular disease, kidney stone formation, and pancreatic cancer. \n\nDiabetes can manifest in different forms, including Type 1, Type 2, gestational diabetes, and MODY, and is a significant area of research and clinical focus, particularly regarding its treatment and management strategies. Patients with diabetes may require specialized dietary formulas designed to modify disease progression and manage their condition effectively. \n\nThe management of diabetes often involves medication, lifestyle changes, and adherence to specific dietary guidelines, such as those influenced by the Mediterranean diet. Additionally, diabetes can complicate the management of other health conditions, such as chronic kidney disease and obesity, and is linked to increased risks of frailty in older adults. \n\nIn some cases, diabetes may arise from hormonal imbalances or be a complication of other metabolic disorders, such as hereditary hemochromatosis or MELAS syndrome. The condition is also noted for its economic impact on healthcare systems and the importance of dietary assessment for prevention and treatment. Overall, diabetes is a complex metabolic disorder that requires comprehensive management to mitigate its effects on health and quality of life.\",163\r\n4123,TRATAMIENTO,\"The term \"\"TRATAMIENTO\"\" encompasses a variety of medical interventions aimed at improving patient health across different conditions. In the case of classical galactosemia, treatment involves the strict elimination of galactose from the diet starting from the first days of life. For oncology patients, treatment may include a combination o